Perfect 🔥 Welcome to Day 5: Object-Oriented Programming (OOP).
This is the day we switch from “just scripts” to thinking like architects — modeling real-world problems with classes and objects.

🧠 Core Concepts for Today

Classes & Objects – blueprints and actual things

Attributes & Methods – data and behavior inside objects

__init__ Constructor – set up objects when created

Encapsulation – protecting data with private attributes (_ / __)

Inheritance – reusing code between classes (Parent → Child)

Polymorphism – methods behaving differently depending on the object

👨‍💻 Day 5 Real-Life Style Tasks
✅ 1. Student Management System

Create a Student class with:

Attributes: name, age, grades (list of numbers)

Methods:

get_average() → returns average grade

has_passed() → returns True if average ≥ 50

Bonus: Create multiple students and store them in a list. Print all who passed.

✅ 2. Bank Account System

Create a BankAccount class with:

Attributes: owner, balance (default = 0)

Methods:

deposit(amount) → adds to balance

withdraw(amount) → subtracts if enough funds, else print “Insufficient balance”

__str__() → nicely print account info

Bonus: Inherit from BankAccount to make a SavingsAccount with an extra method add_interest(rate) that increases balance by a percentage.

✅ 3. Library System

Create a Book class with attributes: title, author, available (default = True)

Create a Library class with:

books (list of Book objects)

add_book(book)

borrow_book(title) → mark as unavailable

return_book(title) → mark as available

Add exception handling if book isn’t found.

✅ 4. Employee Payroll

Create a base class Employee with attributes: name, salary

Create subclasses:

Manager (bonus attribute: department)

Developer (bonus attribute: programming_language)

Add a method get_details() in each class that prints customized info.

✅ 5. Shape Calculator (Polymorphism Demo)

Create a base class Shape with a method area().

Create subclasses:

Rectangle (attributes: length, width → area = length * width)

Circle (attribute: radius → area = π * r^2)

Triangle (attributes: base, height → area = 0.5 * base * height)

Demonstrate polymorphism by looping through a list of shapes and calling .area() on each.

⚡ Bonus Challenge: School System

Create a School class that holds:

A list of Student objects

A list of Teacher objects

Add methods like:

add_student(), add_teacher()

list_students(), list_teachers()

find_student(name) → returns the student object if found

📌 Instructions

Put all tasks into one file (like before)

Use classes and objects everywhere (no free-floating functions unless necessary)

Test your classes with multiple objects (not just one per class)

🔥 This day is a big milestone. Once you’re comfortable with OOP, you’re coding like real software engineers.
